    {"id": "TARGET_INFANTRY_CAMP_SELECTED", "type": "STATUS", "cn": "盾兵营（被选中：金色光环）", "en_aliases": ["infantry camp selected", "gold selection ring"], "ocr": [], "icon_semantic": "TARGET_INFANTRY_CAMP_HIGHLIGHTED", "pages": ["HOME"], "target_state": "SELECTED_BUILDING_CLICKABLE", "actions": ["SELECT_INFANTRY_CAMP"], "status": "VERIFIED", "source": "LIVE_CLIENT", "last_verified": "2026-09-21", "notes": "This is the capability's ONLY identity, and it is a recognition-only semantic: the goal-driven training route reads it to name stage A (highlighted, no radial menu yet) and nothing taps it directly any more.  Whether a tap can act on the camp is decided by a DIFFERENT quantity -- the gold selection ring's size, read off the same frame by winter_agent_v2/camp_ring.py, which taps the ring's centre when a ring is present and answers None otherwise.  Measured on the pair a human verified by hand (dataset/truth_audit/training_camp_highlight_ambiguity_20260917/): the frame whose tap opened the menu carries a 205x112 ring; the frame whose tap jumped to the world map carries a 7x15 fleck of gold -- a badge edge, not a ring.  Two orders of magnitude, and it is the ring that separates the two states, not the template distance (0.0 against 8.0, the latter sitting exactly on the production gate, which is why the older note concluded the signal could not tell them apart).  Over 46 live stage A frames from five sessions the ring measures x 305-319, y 577-597 (sd 4.3/4.0) while this template's own centre is (346, 682) on every one of them -- 103 px away, 37 px below the ring's lower edge, on bare ground, where a tap is a map tap.  Full evidence: dataset/truth_audit/training_stage_a_20260921/README.md."}
